The problem is to determine if a string `s` is a subsequence of another string `t`. A subsequence is a sequence that appears in the same relative order, but not necessarily contiguous.

Here's a Java solution using two pointers:

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isSubsequence(String s, String t) {
        int i = 0;
        for (char c : t.toCharArray()) {
            if (i < s.length() && c == s.charAt(i)) {
                i++;
            }
        }
        return i == s.length();
    }

    public void testIsSubsequence() {
        assertTrue(isSubsequence("abc", "ahbgdc"));
        assertFalse(isSubsequence("axc", "ahbgdc"));
        assertTrue(isSubsequence("", "ahbgdc"));
        assertTrue(isSubsequence("abc", ""));
        assertFalse(isSubsequence("abcd", ""));
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution solution = new Solution();
        solution.testIsSubsequence();
    }
}
```

This code works by iterating through the characters of `t` and checking if they match the current character in `s`. If a match is found, it increments the pointer to keep track of the position in `s`. Finally, it checks if all characters in `s` have been matched.
